    topics: [
      { title: 'Physician (Bhishak) Qualities in Detail', content: 'Shruta: thorough knowledge of Ayurvedic texts (Charak, Sushruta, Ashtanga Hridaya), pharmacology (Dravyaguna), and disease pathology (Roga Vigyan). Drushtakarma: extensive clinical experience under qualified supervision - minimum 5-10 years of supervised practice. Daksha: dexterity in procedures - Panchakarma, surgical techniques, pulse diagnosis. Shuchi: physical cleanliness (sterile technique) and mental purity (ethical conduct, no exploitation of patients).', clinicalRelevance: 'A physician lacking any one of these qualities will have reduced treatment success - continuous professional development is essential.' },
      { title: 'Medicine (Dravya) Qualities in Detail', content: 'Bahuta: sufficient quantity available for complete treatment course - running out mid-treatment disrupts therapy. Yogyatva: suitability for the specific disease and patient - the right drug for the right condition. Anekavidha Kalpana: availability in multiple forms - Kashaya (decoction), Churna (powder), Ghrita (medicated ghee), Taila (oil), Lepa (paste), Vati (tablets). Sampad: authentic quality - correct species, proper harvesting time, correct processing, no adulteration.', clinicalRelevance: 'Medicine quality is often the weakest link in treatment - ensuring authentic, properly processed herbs is critical for clinical outcomes.' },
      { title: 'Attendant (Upasthata) Qualities in Detail', content: 'Upachara Kusala: skilled in all nursing procedures - medicine administration, Panchakarma assistance, wound care, vital signs monitoring. Daksha: alert and responsive to patient needs, quick to identify complications, efficient in procedures. Dakshina: compassionate and caring attitude - treats patient with dignity, provides emotional support, maintains confidentiality. Shuchi: meticulous cleanliness - sterile technique, clean environment, proper hygiene.', clinicalRelevance: 'The attendant spends more time with the patient than the physician - their skills and attitude directly affect treatment compliance and patient satisfaction.' },
      { title: 'Patient (Rogi) Qualities in Detail', content: 'Smriti: good memory - remembers symptoms accurately, follows instructions, takes medicines on time, recalls dietary restrictions. Abhirutva: courage - willing to undergo Panchakarma procedures, accepts diagnosis positively, maintains hope. Gyapti: obedience - follows physician instructions strictly, adheres to dietary and lifestyle modifications, attends follow-ups. Satvava: truthfulness - provides accurate history, reports symptoms honestly, admits non-compliance.', clinicalRelevance: 'Patient qualities are the most variable component - assessing patient compliance capacity before treatment prevents wasted effort and improves outcomes.' },
      { title: 'Asadhya (Incurable) Patient Selection', content: 'Patients who should not be treated: (1) Those with diseases beyond medical treatment (Atyanta Asadhya). (2) Those who refuse treatment or do not comply. (3) Those who are hostile to the physician. (4) Those who cannot afford treatment (practical consideration). (5) Those whose disease is in final stage. (6) Those who lack Smriti, Abhirutva, Gyapti, or Satvava. Knowing when NOT to treat is as important as knowing how to treat.', clinicalRelevance: 'Patient selection protects both patient and physician - attempting treatment on incurable cases wastes resources and may cause harm.' },
    ],
    diseaseDescriptions: [
      { name: 'Chikitsa Asiddhi (Treatment Failure)', sanskrit: 'चिकित्सासिद्धि', etiology: 'Deficiency in one or more Chatushpada components. Physician: lack of knowledge, experience, skill, or purity. Medicine: unavailable, unsuitable, wrong form, or adulterated. Attendant: unskilled, careless, uncaring, or unclean. Patient: forgetful, fearful, disobedient, or dishonest.', symptoms: ['No improvement in symptoms', 'Worsening of disease', 'New symptoms appearing', 'Treatment complications', 'Patient non-compliance', 'Medicine ineffectiveness', 'Physician frustration', 'Attendant burnout'], prognosis: 'Preventable with proper Chatushpada assessment before treatment. Addressable by identifying and strengthening the weak component.', treatment: 'Identify the deficient component: if physician - seek specialist consultation or additional training. If medicine - source better quality herbs or change formulation. If attendant - retrain or replace. If patient - counsel, educate, or consider non-treatment if Asadhya.' },
      { name: 'Vaidya Dosha (Physician Deficiency)', sanskrit: 'वैद्यदोष', etiology: 'Physician lacking one or more of four qualities: Ashruta (inadequate theoretical knowledge), Adrushtakarma (lack of clinical experience), Adaksha (lack of dexterity/skill), Ashuchi (lack of purity - physical or ethical). Also: arrogance, greed, negligence, overconfidence.', symptoms: ['Wrong diagnosis', 'Inappropriate treatment selection', 'Procedure complications', 'Patient harm', 'Treatment failure', 'Loss of patient trust', 'Ethical violations', 'Medicolegal issues'], prognosis: 'Preventable through continuous education, supervised practice, skill development, and ethical conduct.', treatment: 'Continuing medical education, clinical mentorship, skill workshops, ethical counseling, peer review. If irredeemable - recommend change of profession.' },
    ],
